    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Apologies for the confusion. Let's get straight to the technicalities. The angle of attack on the compressor wheel blades is a critical parameter that directly influences the aerodynamic performance of the compressor. When the angle of attack is too high, it can lead to aerodynamic stall, where the blades can no longer effectively capture and compress the air, resulting in a sudden drop in pressure and mass flow rate. This phenomenon is similar to an airplane wing stalling when the angle of attack exceeds a certain limit, causing a loss of lift.\n\nIn the context of a diesel engine, compressor surge occurs when the pressure in the compressor system rises rapidly, causing a reversal of airflow through the compressor. This can lead to severe pressure fluctuations and potentially damage the compressor. The relationship between the blade angle of attack and compressor surge is complex, as it involves the interplay between the aerodynamic forces acting on the blades and the dynamic behavior of the airflow through the compressor.\n\nTo prevent compressor surge, it is essential to carefully design the blade geometry and angle of attack to ensure stable airflow and pressure distribution throughout the compressor. This can be achieved by optimizing the blade shape, size, and angle of attack to minimize aerodynamic stall and pressure fluctuations. Additionally, the use of advanced control systems and sensors can help",
